# Security Practices

## Credential Storage

**Never hardcode credentials.** Store API keys, usernames, passwords, and tokens in:
- Environment variables
- Encrypted settings storage (ISettingsService with encryption)
- User secrets (development only)

```csharp
// ❌ BAD
var apiKey = "sk-1234567890abcdef";

// ✅ GOOD
var apiKey = await _settingsService.GetAsync("ComicVine:ApiKey");
```

## Logging & Error Messages

**Never log sensitive data.** Mask or omit credentials in logs and error messages.

```csharp
// ❌ BAD
_logger.LogError("Auth failed for user {User} with password {Password}", username, password);

// ✅ GOOD
_logger.LogError("Auth failed for user {User}", username);
```

## API Responses

**Never expose credentials in API responses.** Use separate DTOs for input vs output.

```csharp
// ❌ BAD - Returns password in response
public record SettingsResponse(string Username, string Password);

// ✅ GOOD - Password write-only, masked in response
public record SettingsResponse(string Username, bool IsConfigured);
```

## Frontend Handling

**Never store credentials in localStorage or expose in URLs.**

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
localStorage.setItem('apiKey', key);
window.location.href = `/api?key=${apiKey}`;

// ✅ GOOD
// Send credentials only in request body over HTTPS
await api.updateSettings({ apiKey });
```

## Password Fields

**Use appropriate input types and prevent autocomplete for sensitive fields.**

```tsx
// ✅ GOOD
<input type="password" autoComplete="new-password" />
```

## Validation

**Validate credentials server-side before enabling features.**

```csharp
// ✅ GOOD - Reject enable without credentials
if (request.Enabled && string.IsNullOrEmpty(settings.Username))
    return Results.BadRequest("Cannot enable without credentials");
```
